window.docContents[186].resolve({"/Elementary-Functions/___4___10-Integrals/#LMLF-___-DLMF-with-Lean--Elementary-Functions--___4___10-Integrals--___4___10_LPAR_i_RPAR_-Logarithms":{"contents":"\\int\\frac{dz}{z}=\\ln z.On a region avoiding zero and the principal branch cut, the derivative of the\nprincipal logarithm is 1/z.\n\n\\int\\ln z\\,dz=z\\ln z-z.Differentiating z ln z − z gives the principal logarithm on the same branch\ndomain.\n\n\\int z^n\\ln z\\,dz=\\frac{z^{n+1}}{n+1}\\ln z-\\frac{z^{n+1}}{(n+1)^2},\\qquad n\\ne-1.For an integer exponent other than −1, integration by parts yields the\ndisplayed logarithmic power antiderivative on a chosen principal branch.\n\n\\int\\frac{dz}{z\\ln z}=\\ln(\\ln z).Where both z and its principal logarithm are nonzero, the iterated logarithm\nhas derivative 1/(z ln z) on the selected branch.\n\n\\int_0^1\\frac{\\ln t}{1-t}\\,dt=-\\frac{\\pi^2}{6}.The improper endpoint integral over (0,1) has the stated negative zeta-value.\n\n\\int_0^1\\frac{\\ln t}{1+t}\\,dt=-\\frac{\\pi^2}{12}.The corresponding improper integral with denominator 1+t equals\n−π²/12.\n\n\\operatorname{PV}\\!\\int_0^x\\frac{dt}{\\ln t}=\\operatorname{li}(x),\\qquad x>1.For x>1, the logarithmic integral is interpreted as a Cauchy principal value\nat the interior singularity t=1; the local vocabulary records that symmetric\ncutoff interpretation.\n\n","context":"LMLF · DLMF with Lean\u0009Elementary Functions\u0009§4.10 Integrals","header":"§4.10(i) Logarithms","id":"/Elementary-Functions/___4___10-Integrals/#LMLF-___-DLMF-with-Lean--Elementary-Functions--___4___10-Integrals--___4___10_LPAR_i_RPAR_-Logarithms"}});